{
  "name": "nist_csf_v2",
  "annotations": [
    {
      "tag": "cprt_framework_version",
      "value": "CSF_2_0_0"
    },
    {
      "tag": "publication_date",
      "value": "2024-02"
    },
    {
      "tag": "oscal_content_tag",
      "value": "v1.6.0"
    },
    {
      "tag": "oscal_content_commit",
      "value": "f0de6d8d8c059d374f15e0ae07508d82e370fc1d"
    }
  ],
  "description": "A LinkML model of the Cybersecurity Framework (CSF) 2.0, which NIST publishes as Cybersecurity White Paper (CSWP) 29: its six functions, their categories and subcategories, the implementation examples NIST publishes with the subcategories, and the CSF 1.1 categories and subcategories that CSF 2.0 withdrew, each with its status and, where NIST recorded it, where it moved or what incorporated it.",
  "title": "The NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF) 2.0",
  "comments": [
    "The version is the CSF edition that CSWP 29 defines, and source is the publication's digital object identifier. NIST's Cybersecurity and Privacy Reference Tool names the same edition CSF_2_0_0, and the catalog NIST publishes for it in usnistgov/oscal-content is copied to src/nist_csf_v2/schema/oscal-content/ at the tag and commit in the annotations. That catalog carries a version number of its own, 1.2.1, which numbers the catalog and not the framework. The release tags are the schema's own versions, and scripts/check_upstream_release.py reads the same pins and reports when NIST publishes another edition or changes the catalog.",
    "CSWP 29 is a work of the United States Government and is not subject to copyright in the United States. The descriptions here are this project's own words; NIST's text of each function, category, subcategory and example stays in the catalog copy.",
    "NIST's catalog renders each function as an OSCAL group, each category and subcategory as an OSCAL control and each implementation example as a part. The broad_mappings name those lmodel/oscal classes. A withdrawn category or subcategory is a control with a status property and links to where it moved or what incorporated it; the schema carries those as the element's status and identifiers.",
    "The catalog also marks most CSF 2.0 subcategories as first-party risk, third-party risk or both, in a marking property of class risk-party. CSWP 29 carries no such marking, so it is the reference tool's and the schema leaves it out."
  ],
  "source": "https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.CSWP.29",
  "see_also": [
    "https://lmodel.github.io/nist-csf-v2",
    "https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/cswp/29/the-nist-cybersecurity-framework-csf-20/final",
    "https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework",
    "https://github.com/usnistgov/oscal-content/tree/v1.6.0/nist.gov/CSF/v2.0"
  ],
  "id": "https://w3id.org/lmodel/nist-csf-v2",
  "version": "2.0.0",
  "imports": [
    "linkml:types"
  ],
  "license": "Apache-2.0",
  "prefixes": [
    {
      "prefix_prefix": "linkml",
      "prefix_reference": "https://w3id.org/linkml/"
    },
    {
      "prefix_prefix": "nist_csf_v2",
      "prefix_reference": "https://w3id.org/lmodel/nist-csf-v2/"
    },
    {
      "prefix_prefix": "oscal",
      "prefix_reference": "https://w3id.org/lmodel/oscal/"
    },
    {
      "prefix_prefix": "schema",
      "prefix_reference": "http://schema.org/"
    },
    {
      "prefix_prefix": "xsd",
      "prefix_reference": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#"
    }
  ],
  "default_prefix": "nist_csf_v2",
  "default_range": "string",
  "types": [
    {
      "name": "csf_function_identifier",
      "definition_uri": "https://w3id.org/lmodel/nist-csf-v2/CsfFunctionIdentifier",
      "description": "The two letters that name one of the six functions, such as GV for GOVERN.",
      "from_schema": "https://w3id.org/lmodel/nist-csf-v2",
      "typeof": "string",
      "base": "str",
      "uri": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string",
      "pattern": "^(GV|ID|PR|DE|RS|RC)$",
      "@type": "TypeDefinition"
    },
    {
      "name": "csf_category_identifier",
      "definition_uri": "https://w3id.org/lmodel/nist-csf-v2/CsfCategoryIdentifier",
      "description": "A category's identifier: its function's letters, a dot and two letters for the category, such as GV.OC for Organizational Context.",
      "from_schema": "https://w3id.org/lmodel/nist-csf-v2",
      "typeof": "string",
      "base": "str",
      "uri": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string",
      "pattern": "^(GV|ID|PR|DE|RS|RC)\\.[A-Z]{2}$",
      "@type": "TypeDefinition"
    },
    {
      "name": "csf_subcategory_identifier",
      "definition_uri": "https://w3id.org/lmodel/nist-csf-v2/CsfSubcategoryIdentifier",
      "description": "A subcategory's identifier: its category's identifier, a hyphen and a two-digit number, such as GV.OC-01. CSWP 29 says the numbers are not sequential on purpose: a gap marks a CSF 1.1 subcategory that CSF 2.0 moved elsewhere.",
      "from_schema": "https://w3id.org/lmodel/nist-csf-v2",
      "typeof": "string",
      "base": "str",
      "uri": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string",
      "pattern": "^(GV|ID|PR|DE|RS|RC)\\.[A-Z]{2}-[0-9]{2}$",
      "@type": "TypeDefinition"
    },
    {
      "name": "csf_implementation_example_identifier",
      "definition_uri": "https://w3id.org/lmodel/nist-csf-v2/CsfImplementationExampleIdentifier",
      "description": "The identifier NIST's reference data gives an implementation example: its subcategory's identifier, a dot and a three-digit number, such as GV.OC-01.001. The numbers run once through the whole Core, so no two examples share one.",
      "from_schema": "https://w3id.org/lmodel/nist-csf-v2",
      "typeof": "string",
      "base": "str",
      "uri": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string",
      "pattern": "^(GV|ID|PR|DE|RS|RC)\\.[A-Z]{2}-[0-9]{2}\\.[0-9]{3}$",
      "@type": "TypeDefinition"
    },
    {
      "name": "csf_element_identifier",
      "definition_uri": "https://w3id.org/lmodel/nist-csf-v2/CsfElementIdentifier",
      "description": "The identifier of a function, a category or a subcategory, whichever of the three a withdrawn element names as what incorporated it.",
      "from_schema": "https://w3id.org/lmodel/nist-csf-v2",
      "typeof": "string",
      "base": "str",
      "uri": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string",
      "pattern": "^(GV|ID|PR|DE|RS|RC)(\\.[A-Z]{2}(-[0-9]{2})?)?$",
      "@type": "TypeDefinition"
    },
    {
      "name": "string",
      "definition_uri": "https://w3id.org/linkml/String",
      "description": "A character string",
      "notes": [
        "In RDF serializations, a slot with range of string is treated as a literal or type xsd:string.   If you are authoring schemas in LinkML YAML, the type is referenced with the lower case \"string\"."
      ],
      "from_schema": "https://w3id.org/linkml/types",
      "imported_from": "linkml:types",
      "exact_mappings": [
        "schema:Text"
      ],
      "base": "str",
      "uri": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string",
      "@type": "TypeDefinition"
    },
    {
      "name": "integer",
      "definition_uri": "https://w3id.org/linkml/Integer",
      "description": "An integer",
      "notes": [
        "If you are authoring schemas in LinkML YAML, the type is referenced with the lower case \"integer\"."
      ],
      "from_schema": "https://w3id.org/linkml/types",
      "imported_from": "linkml:types",
      "exact_mappings": [
        "schema:Integer"
      ],
      "base": "int",
      "uri": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer",
      "@type": "TypeDefinition"
    },
    {
